CAT = "auwhiteHealth"; SEG = "HLT"
Q = [
("Since when has all use of asbestos been banned in Australia?",
 "31 December 2003", ["31 December 1983", "31 December 1990", "31 December 2013"],
 "A national ban on using, importing and selling all forms of asbestos began on 31 December 2003. Asbestos already in buildings was not removed by the ban, so it is still found on sites."),
("Which buildings are most likely to contain asbestos?",
 "Those built before 1990", ["Those built after 2010", "Only old factories", "Only rural buildings"],
 "Homes and buildings built before 1990 are very likely to contain asbestos, and any built before the end of 2003 may. It was used in sheeting, roofing, flooring, pipes and insulation."),
("You uncover material you think might be asbestos. What should you do?",
 "Stop work and tell your supervisor", ["Wet it down and keep working", "Bag it up and put it in the site skip", "Sweep it up straight away"],
 "Stop, keep others away and report it so the material can be identified and handled by the right people. Disturbing it can release fibres."),
("What does an asbestos register record?",
 "Where asbestos is in the building", ["Who removed asbestos last year", "Workers' chest X-ray results", "Removalists in the local area"],
 "An asbestos register lists identified or assumed asbestos in a workplace, its location, type and condition. Check it before drilling, cutting or demolishing anything in an older building."),
("Which disease is caused by breathing in asbestos fibres?",
 "Mesothelioma", ["Silicosis", "Tetanus", "Legionnaires' disease"],
 "Asbestos fibres can cause mesothelioma, asbestosis and lung cancer. Silicosis comes from crystalline silica dust instead."),
("How long after exposure can asbestos-related diseases appear?",
 "20 to 40 years or more", ["Within a few days", "Within 6 months", "Within 1 to 2 years at most"],
 "Asbestos diseases usually show up decades after exposure, so feeling fine now proves nothing. There is no known safe level of exposure."),
("Removing friable asbestos needs which licence?",
 "A Class A asbestos licence", ["A Class B asbestos licence", "A White Card only", "A demolition licence"],
 "Only a Class A licensed removalist may remove friable asbestos. A Class B licence covers non-friable asbestos only."),
("Non-friable asbestos over what area must be removed by a licensed removalist?",
 "10 square metres", ["1 square metre", "5 square metres", "50 square metres"],
 "Removing more than 10 square metres of non-friable asbestos needs at least a Class B licence. Smaller amounts still need safe methods and trained workers."),
("Which of these must never be used on asbestos-containing material?",
 "A high-pressure water blaster", ["A damp disposable rag", "A low-pressure garden sprayer", "Heavy plastic sheeting"],
 "High-pressure water, compressed air, brooms and most power tools spread fibres, so they are banned on asbestos with only narrow exceptions. Low-pressure wetting helps keep fibres down."),
("What does 'friable' asbestos mean?",
 "It crumbles easily by hand", ["It is bonded in cement", "It is sealed with paint", "It is buried underground"],
 "Friable asbestos is in powder form or can be crumbled or reduced to powder by hand pressure when dry. It releases fibres easily, which is why it needs a Class A licence."),
("Cutting which of these can release harmful crystalline silica dust?",
 "Concrete", ["Untreated pine", "Copper pipe", "PVC pipe"],
 "Concrete, bricks, mortar, tiles and stone all contain crystalline silica. Cutting, grinding or drilling them releases fine dust that can be breathed deep into the lungs."),
("Breathing in crystalline silica dust over time can cause:",
 "Silicosis", ["Mesothelioma", "Tinnitus", "Dermatitis"],
 "Silicosis is an incurable scarring of the lungs caused by respirable crystalline silica. Mesothelioma is linked to asbestos."),
("What has been banned in Australia since 1 July 2024?",
 "Engineered stone products", ["Natural granite benchtops", "Ceramic floor tiles", "Fibre cement sheeting"],
 "The manufacture, supply, processing and installation of engineered stone benchtops, panels and slabs is banned because cutting it caused a wave of silicosis. Work on engineered stone already installed is tightly controlled."),
("What is the best way to control silica dust when cutting concrete?",
 "Wet cutting or on-tool extraction", ["Dry cutting outside in a breeze", "Sweeping all the dust up afterwards", "Cutting quickly to limit time"],
 "Water suppression or on-tool dust extraction captures dust at the source, usually with a respirator as well. Wind and sweeping spread the dust around."),
("How should silica dust be cleaned up?",
 "Wet methods or an H-class vacuum", ["Dry sweeping with a broom", "Blowing it off with compressed air", "Shaking out the drop sheets"],
 "Wet wiping or an H-class vacuum captures fine dust. Dry sweeping and compressed air throw it back into the air you breathe."),
("Why must you be clean-shaven to wear a tight-fitting respirator?",
 "Stubble breaks the face seal", ["Hair clogs up the filter", "It is a site dress rule", "Beards cause skin rashes"],
 "A tight-fitting respirator only works if it seals to the skin, and even short stubble lets dust leak in around it. Fit testing checks the seal."),
("What is a safety data sheet (SDS)?",
 "Safety information for a chemical", ["A daily sign-in and attendance sheet", "A plant pre-start checklist", "An incident report form"],
 "An SDS describes a chemical's hazards, safe handling and storage, PPE, first aid and spill procedures. It must be available to workers who use the chemical."),
("Who must prepare the SDS for a hazardous chemical?",
 "Its manufacturer or importer", ["The site supervisor", "The worker who uses it on site", "The local council"],
 "The manufacturer or importer prepares the SDS, and the supplier must provide it. The PCBU must get it and make it available to workers."),
("How often must a safety data sheet be reviewed?",
 "At least every 5 years", ["Every year", "Every 10 years", "Only when it is lost or damaged"],
 "Under the model WHS Regulations a manufacturer or importer must review and update an SDS at least once every 5 years, or sooner if new information comes to light."),
("You pour a hazardous chemical into a smaller container to use later. What must it have?",
 "A label with its name and hazards", ["No label if you'll use it this week", "Just the date it was poured", "Only the supplier's phone number"],
 "Decanted chemicals must be labelled with at least the product name and its hazards unless used straight away and the container cleaned. Unlabelled containers lead to poisonings and burns."),
("On a hazardous chemical label, which signal word shows the more severe hazard?",
 "Danger", ["Warning", "Caution", "Notice"],
 "Labels use one of two signal words: 'Danger' for the more severe hazards and 'Warning' for the less severe."),
("A red dangerous goods diamond with a flame and the number 3 marks:",
 "Flammable liquids", ["Toxic and infectious substances", "Corrosives", "Explosives"],
 "Class 3 dangerous goods are flammable liquids such as petrol, solvents and some paints. Keep them away from heat, sparks and naked flames."),
("A dangerous goods diamond with class number 8 marks:",
 "Corrosive substances", ["Flammable gases", "Explosives", "Radioactive material"],
 "Class 8 is corrosive substances, such as some acids and alkalis, which can burn skin and eyes and damage metal. Check the SDS for PPE before handling."),
("How should flammable liquids be stored on site?",
 "Away from ignition sources", ["Next to the site generator", "In the lunch room fridge", "In old drink bottles"],
 "Store flammable liquids in labelled, approved containers in a ventilated area away from heat, sparks and hot work. Drink bottles invite someone to swallow them."),
("Which of these is a confined space?",
 "A sewer access pit", ["A site office", "An open-sided shed", "A scaffold platform"],
 "A confined space is enclosed or partly enclosed, not designed for people to work in and may have unsafe air or engulfment risks. Pits, tanks, sewers, silos and pipes are typical examples."),
("What must be in place before a worker enters a confined space?",
 "A confined space entry permit", ["A high risk work licence", "A verbal OK from an experienced workmate", "A hot work permit by itself"],
 "Entry needs a written entry permit, set out by a competent person, covering air testing, isolation, ventilation, the standby person and rescue arrangements."),
("What does the standby person do during confined space entry?",
 "Stays outside and monitors", ["Enters first to test the air", "Leaves once workers are in", "Works inside alongside them"],
 "The standby person stays outside, keeps watch and in contact, and starts rescue procedures if something goes wrong, without entering unless trained and equipped to do so."),
("A worker collapses inside a pit. What should you do?",
 "Raise the alarm; don't go in", ["Climb straight in and pull them out", "Lower water down to them", "Wait to see if they wake"],
 "The air that dropped the first person can drop the rescuer, and would-be rescuers die in confined space incidents. Call for help and follow the rescue plan."),
]
